response_evidence:
GET /api/Feedbacks (unauthenticated) returned HTTP 200 with JSON containing 8 feedback records, e.g.:
{"UserId":1,"id":1,"comment":"I love this shop! Best products in town! Highly recommended! (***in@juice-sh.op)", ...}
{"UserId":2,"id":2,"comment":"Great shop! Awesome service! (***@juice-sh.op)", ...}
{"UserId":3,"id":3,"comment":"Nothing useful available here! (***der@juice-sh.op)", ...}
{"UserId":21,"id":4,"comment":"...(***ereum@juice-sh.op)", ...}

While the endpoint is indeed unauthenticated and does return UserId linkage plus an email-domain fragment embedded in each comment, the local-part of every email is masked with literal "***" characters (e.g. "***in@juice-sh.op", "***@juice-sh.op") rather than disclosing the full plaintext author email address. The verification criterion requires the endpoint to "disclose author email addresses" -- since no record observed contains a complete, unredacted email address, this does not conclusively satisfy the CWE-359 behavioral proof as specified. Marking inconclusive rather than confirmed/rejected: recommend Coordinator determine whether partial/masked email fragments combined with UserId (enabling correlation/enumeration) should still count as PII exposure, or whether a different endpoint/version exposes full addresses.
